<mujoco model="ur5e_pendulum_attachment">
  <!--
  Standalone MJCF for the physical "UR5e Pendulum v1" apparatus
  (cartpole_cad/UR5e Pendulum v1.zip) -- the free-swinging "pole" this
  workspace's original "real_Cartpole" name refers to, mounted at the
  UR5e's wrist_3 attachment_site via an OnRobot tool-changer.

  Meant to be composed onto the existing, PROTECTED centerpiece model
  (assets/ur5e_torque/ur5e_torque.xml, see AGENTS.md sec 2 -- "never
  delete or silently regenerate it") via mujoco.MjSpec.attach(site=...)
  at that model's "attachment_site" -- see
  simulation/ur5e_pendulum_compose.py for the loader. This file is NEVER
  included via plain <include> into ur5e_torque.xml or its scene.xml;
  it has no <worldbody> root itself so it cannot be loaded standalone as
  a top-level model (this is intentional -- it is an attachment
  fragment, not a scene).

  ============================================================
  DIMENSION PROVENANCE -- read before trusting any number below
  ============================================================
  The CAD (cartpole_cad/UR5e Pendulum v1.zip) is a genuine SolidWorks
  assembly (UR5e Pendulum v1.SLDASM) with real custom parts (Pendulum
  Rod, Rod Housing, Shaft Housing, Sensor Housing, Linear Motion Rod,
  8mm Bore Clamp) plus catalog hardware (McMaster fasteners, an MF128ZZ
  flanged ball bearing) and a CUI/Same Sky AMT222B-8000-S single-turn
  absolute magnetic encoder assembly. SolidWorks .SLDPRT/.SLDASM files
  are a proprietary binary format this environment has no reader for
  (no SolidWorks, no working translator found -- cadquery/OCP was
  installed and DOES read the two open-format .STEP files in the
  archive, but not the native SolidWorks parts).

  What is REAL (extracted or directly inferred from the archive):
    - OnRobot tool-changer robot-side plate: 71mm x 71mm x 16.1mm
      (bounding box, read via cadquery from
      "ST-RB-028-0004 OnRobot Tool Changer (Robot Side).STEP").
    - Tool-side plate bounding box: 75.51mm x 28.40mm x 71.00mm (same
      method, "...0023...Tool Side.STEP").
    - Rod/shaft diameter = 8mm -- not measured, but two independently
      named parts in the archive reference "8mm" explicitly ("8mm Bore
      Clamp", "Same_Sky_AMT-Sleeve-REV-D(8 mm)"), a real, non-arbitrary
      signal for this one dimension.
    - The encoder is a SINGLE-TURN ABSOLUTE type (CUI/Same Sky AMT22
      series) -- confirmed by part family, exact bit-resolution not
      confirmed (the "-8000-S" suffix was not decoded with confidence,
      not asserted here).

  What is PLACEHOLDER (physically reasonable, NOT measured -- flagged
  explicitly on every such value below, must be corrected against the
  real CAD/hardware before this model is used for anything beyond a
  first smoke test):
    - Pendulum rod LENGTH (0.30 m assumed -- a common lab-scale choice,
      no support from the archive's file list or metadata).
    - Rod/housing MASS and material density (assumed mild steel,
      7850 kg/m^3, since "Linear Motion Rod" + a ball-bearing pivot
      reads as a hardened-steel linear/rotary shaft component, but
      this is an inference, not a measurement).
    - Pivot joint AXIS ORIENTATION relative to attachment_site -- chosen
      here so the rod hangs down under gravity from a horizontal start
      in this file's own smoke test (tools/diagnostics/
      ur5e_pendulum_smoke_test.py), NOT derived from the CAD assembly's
      actual mate definitions (which are unreadable). If the real
      mechanism's swing plane differs, this needs correcting.
    - No discrete "bob" end-mass is modeled -- the archive's part list
      has no separate weighted part beyond the rod/housings, so mass is
      modeled as distributed along a uniform rod plus a small lumped
      mass for the encoder/housing cluster near the pivot. If the real
      assembly has a separate end weight, this is wrong and needs a
      dedicated body added.

  Do not treat any PLACEHOLDER value as ground truth for anything beyond
  a first-pass kinematic/dynamic sanity check.
  -->

  <default>
    <default class="pendulum">
      <joint damping="0.02" frictionloss="0.01"/>
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" density="7850"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <!-- Real OnRobot tool-changer meshes (added 2026-08-10), replacing the
       cylinder-pair placeholder. Exported from the archive's two REAL,
       open-format STEP files (cadquery, meters, coarsened tessellation --
       full-tolerance export was 194789 faces for the tool-side plate alone,
       too heavy to recompile on every test run this session already does
       hundreds of times; this version is 12198/36872 faces).
       ORIENTATION IS AN ASSUMPTION, NOT VERIFIED: each STEP file's Z-axis
       is treated as its thickness/mounting-face-normal axis, matching the
       cylinder placeholder's own convention (robot-side Z-extent = 16.1mm,
       exactly its documented plate thickness -- a real signal this is
       right; tool-side is weaker evidence, its shortest dimension (28.4mm)
       is Y, not Z, meaning it is NOT a simple flat plate along Z the way
       robot-side is). If the real mounting orientation is confirmed wrong,
       fix here, not by returning to the cylinder pair. -->
  <asset>
    <mesh name="toolchanger_robot_side_mesh" file="meshes/toolchanger_robot_side.stl"/>
    <mesh name="toolchanger_tool_side_mesh" file="meshes/toolchanger_tool_side.stl"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="pendulum_mount" pos="0 -0.11 0.08">
      <!-- OnRobot tool-changer stack: REAL bounding-box dimensions
           (see provenance note above). Modeled as a simple flanged
           cylinder pair, not the real tool-changer geometry (STEP
           files were read for bounding box only, not full mesh
           extraction -- a real mesh import is a follow-up, not done
           here).

           pos="0 -0.11 0.08" (2026-08-10, agent-derived, replacing an
           earlier "0 0 0.30" pure-Z attempt that was WRONG -- see below).
           PLACEHOLDER, not a CAD-verified standoff (only 2 of the ~15
           custom parts have STEP files this environment can read; the
           mount-to-pivot distance was already an unmeasured placeholder
           before this fix). Every number quoted here was measured with
           mujoco.mj_geomDistance and forward kinematics at ARM_Q0 =
           [0, -pi/2, pi/2, -pi/2, -pi/2, 0], swept over the pendulum
           hinge, NOT hand-derived.

           FRAME SEMANTICS -- the reason this looks unintuitive. At
           ARM_Q0 the mount frame is NOT axis-aligned with the world
           (measured, do not assume): local +Z points world DOWN, local
           +Y (= the hinge axis) points world +X, local +X points world
           +Y. So this offset means "8cm further down, 11cm out along the
           hinge axis away from the base."

           WHY NOT A PURE local-Z OFFSET. With zero offset the assembly
           genuinely intersects wrist_2_link/wrist_3_link (a real
           geometric fact: ~8mm static tool-changer overlap plus 4.4cm of
           rod penetration near the balance target). Pushing along local
           +Z alone does fix that, but only at >=0.25m, because that is
           what it takes to keep the inverted rod tip BELOW the wrist --
           and local +Z is world DOWN here, while the flange sits at only
           world Z~0.488m. At 0.30m the hub ends up 11.7cm off the floor
           and the hanging rod tip reaches world Z ~ -0.18m, i.e. THROUGH
           the ground plane. That was the state of this file earlier
           today and it was wrong; the floor caps any pure-Z offset at
           ~0.087m, far below the 0.25m the wrist needs. Clearance must
           therefore be bought laterally, and only along local Y: the rod
           sweeps a disc of its own 0.30m length, so an offset within the
           swing plane (local X) never escapes the wrist no matter how
           large (measured: rod clearance pinned at -4.4cm for every
           local-X offset tried, 0.08-0.22m, with or without local Z),
           whereas a local-Y offset translates the whole swing plane off
           the arm.

           MEASURED MARGINS at this value (thresholds: >2cm clearance,
           >3cm above the floor): minimum distance from every arm "link"
           geom to the rod/hub/both tool-changer geoms is 6.25cm, and
           that is the minimum over the FULL +-180deg swing, not just the
           +-60deg window around the balance target (the binding pair,
           wrist_3_link vs the rod, is theta-independent here). Lowest
           world Z reached by the hub or the tip over the full swing is
           3.70cm, at the hanging pose.

           CAVEAT, measured and deliberately not hidden: the feasible box
           is small -- local Y outside [-0.11, -0.09] or local Z off
           0.08 fails one constraint or the other -- and although the
           validated LQR gains do still stabilize this geometry (0
           unstable closed-loop eigenvalues; 0.05-0.40 rad perturbations
           all converge, |final theta error| <= 0.019 rad), they do so
           while riding the torque limits: shoulder_pan and wrist_3 sit
           at/over their clip values on ~90% of control cycles here,
           versus ~3-5% for a pure-Z geometry. Root cause is structural,
           not a tuning miss: a local-Y offset takes the hub off the
           wrist_3 rotation axis, which hands wrist_3 direct control
           authority over the pendulum's swing direction, and these
           gains (q_arm_pos=2655 vs q_pend_angle=20.9, i.e. a very stiff
           arm-hold) then chatter against it. The onset was mapped --
           clean at |local Y| <= 0.05, chattering from ~0.06 -- and the
           rod needs >=0.066 to clear, so the clean region and the
           collision-free region genuinely do not overlap for a 0.30m
           rod at this arm pose. Shortening the rod does not rescue it
           (cleanliness degrades below ~0.26m; swept 0.12-0.45m). Fixing
           that properly needs a gain redesign for this geometry, which
           is a controller question, not a mount-placement one.

           This is a pure translation (no rotation), so it does not move
           the pendulum's own equilibria: gravity torque about the hinge
           is translation-invariant, and the unstable equilibrium is
           exactly theta = -pi both before and after (verified with
           find_inverted_angle, which must still be re-run after any
           geometry change rather than assumed). -->
      <!-- pos values below translate each mesh's OWN bounding-box center to
           where the old placeholder cylinder's center used to sit (0,0,0)
           and (0,0,0.0355) respectively -- i.e. same overall footprint/
           position as before, real shape instead of a cylinder. Mesh
           bbox centers (meters, in each STEP file's native frame): robot
           side (0, 0, 0.00555), tool side (0.00225, 0.01420, 0). -->
      <geom name="toolchanger_robot_side" type="mesh" mesh="toolchanger_robot_side_mesh"
            pos="0 0 -0.00555" class="pendulum" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <geom name="toolchanger_tool_side" type="mesh" mesh="toolchanger_tool_side_mesh"
            pos="-0.00225 -0.01420 0.0355" class="pendulum" rgba="0.3 0.3 0.32 1"/>

      <!-- Encoder/sensor housing cluster (Shaft Housing + Sensor Housing
           + AMT222 assembly), lumped as one small mass near the pivot.
           PLACEHOLDER mass (0.06 kg) -- order-of-magnitude guess for a
           small encoder + housing stack, not a measured value. -->
      <body name="pendulum_hub" pos="0 0 0.071">
        <geom name="pendulum_hub_geom" type="cylinder" size="0.020 0.015" class="pendulum" rgba="0.15 0.15 0.18 1" mass="0.06"/>

        <!-- The single swing DOF -- represents the AMT222 absolute
             encoder's measured axis. Axis choice is a PLACEHOLDER (see
             provenance note); tuned only so the rod hangs under gravity
             in this file's own smoke test, not derived from the real
             assembly's mate geometry. Re-derived 2026-08-11 for the new
             "hanging" transport pose family: at that pose,
             attachment_site's local Y axis maps to world Z (vertical) --
             a hinge about it gets zero gravity torque at any angle, so it
             cannot function as a pendulum at all (found empirically: the
             rod stayed frozen under X-oscillation forcing). local X maps
             to world-horizontal there instead, so axis is now "1 0 0".
             This choice is POSE-DEPENDENT -- re-verify (or re-derive) this
             axis again if the arm's transport pose family changes. -->
        <joint name="pendulum_hinge" type="hinge" axis="1 0 0" pos="0 0 0" class="pendulum" range="-3.14159 3.14159" limited="false"/>
        <site name="pendulum_hinge_site" pos="0 0 0" size="0.004" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>

        <!-- Pendulum rod: 8mm diameter is a real, archive-supported
             value; 0.30m length and steel density are PLACEHOLDERS
             (see provenance note). Rod extends along local +Z from the
             pivot -- the SAME direction the tool-changer stack above
             extends away from attachment_site -- so theta=0 means
             "hanging," matching this file's original documented intent
             (was local -Z before 2026-08-10; see git history).

             IMPORTANT, learned the hard way while fixing the wrist
             collision above: this direction choice does NOT by itself
             affect collision clearance. Gravity's "up" direction is
             fixed in world space, so the "inverted"/balance
             configuration always requires the rod to point the SAME
             physical direction (back toward the wrist side, at this
             apparatus's arm pose) no matter which raw theta value is
             chosen to label it "0" -- flipping this direction only
             relabels which number means "hanging" vs "inverted," it
             does not change where the physical collision risk is. The
             ACTUAL collision fix is the mount's "0 -0.11 0.08" offset
             above (2026-08-10); re-verify against that comment before
             assuming this direction choice matters for clearance. The
             same symmetry is why the FLOOR constraint cannot be dodged
             by flipping this either -- the rod points physically
             downward at one end of the swing regardless of labelling,
             which is exactly what caps the mount's local-Z component.
             mass computed from
             density x volume by the compiler (no explicit mass
             override), consistent with the class="pendulum" default
             density above. -->
        <body name="pendulum_rod" pos="0 0 0">
          <geom name="pendulum_rod_geom" type="cylinder" fromto="0 0 0  0 0 0.30" size="0.004" class="pendulum" rgba="0.6 0.6 0.65 1"/>
          <site name="pendulum_tip_site" pos="0 0 0.30" size="0.006" rgba="0 1 0 1"/>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <sensor>
    <!-- Mirrors the real AMT222's role: a single-axis absolute angle
         reading of pendulum_hinge. jointpos on a hinge joint returns
         the joint's own signed angle in radians -- this is the
         simulated equivalent of what the real encoder reports over its
         digital interface (exact resolution/interface not modeled). -->
    <jointpos name="pendulum_angle_sensor" joint="pendulum_hinge"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
